tate.json" "file_sha256" "50459f288de63b68678b5cd50d67e9bef576e2c3c7d1404b817e2765ab1c3bad" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-x)^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/state.json" "file_sha256" "50459f288de63b68678b5cd50d67e9bef576e2c3c7d1404b817e2765ab1c3bad" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "719d109e0f263a23944557d97e05a7591b3925e13f943ab431df7e88d5f39d5f" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-c