\\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10897.json" "file_sha256" "53d9b661ff2fab2c297c99ecd3e98aa1cebe0aafe80a8ac46490a4d89446c4f8" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10897.json" "file_sha256" "53d9b661ff2fab2c297c99ecd3e98aa1cebe0aafe80a8ac46490a4d89446c4f8" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10898.json" "file_sha256" "65a7a82b412a4888643a2cc519ffc9901c00815fb0a7bf74b77055957d9514d6" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10898.json" "file_sha256" "65a7a82b412a4888643a2cc519ffc9901c00815fb0a7bf74b77055957d9514d6" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "0d08b282b42d4238e9813bbf0fb3765d4f1f75bbfe069eaf2b06fe5c3dc72243" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "0d08b282b42d4238e9813bbf0fb3765d4f1f75bbfe069eaf2b06fe5c3dc72243" "basis" 1182 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014138/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c1ec128486d1779cde54681fee1a120288070b2589fe0b7e6859e2636d19e74" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral I is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n