in{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{4}\\,{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡((-x)^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-88585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-115263" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/140