unction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-88208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-88208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral I is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-115275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi }{3}}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi }{3}}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\frac{\\pi }{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi }{3}}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-334363" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334363" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ A+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-A"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}A"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "A"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|A|"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156191" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-A"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{|-x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={e}^{|x|}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-334383" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334383" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-p"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{-\\sqrt[3]{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334381" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-334381" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-334381" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-p"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-x)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c002ce762d4568a9d06022c78795386c23fbe7302eb25e745b08c2161835273c"} {"question" "q-156690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "q-156690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/state.json" "file_sha256" "b16114cdcc678f245d2b09fb714159dc136a226fa15657b82883b9be7bc2c9c5"} {"question" "e-10897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/example-10897.json" "file_sha256" "6beadedc3eb21bb8103166bfb9581b2661cf74be0d41bd96f05b1e1cb2292b22"} {"question" "e-10897" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/example-10897.json" "file_sha256" "6beadedc3eb21bb8103166bfb9581b2661cf74be0d41bd96f05b1e1cb2292b22"} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/example-10898.json" "file_sha256" "2feb58b40d68ff5d01748c88505125479db242d08fa94b59ef74d5e44af4a6a2"} {"question" "e-10898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/example-10898.json" "file_sha256" "2feb58b40d68ff5d01748c88505125479db242d08fa94b59ef74d5e44af4a6a2"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "73fcabcce9aacdabe9ced2be0d72be15d864d6a4a590e31ec85707f7dcfcacfa"} {"question" "e-10911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/example-10911.json" "file_sha256" "73fcabcce9aacdabe9ced2be0d72be15d864d6a4a590e31ec85707f7dcfcacfa"} {"question" "q-88686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041046/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "6893f2e28e1761a570956682ad2cc2b9dfecf17c0771c0697585c6ecfff5a3b5" "reviewer_session" "manual-source-key-review-practical-1791473061060439493" "rationale" "Confidence here concerns accurately reading the source solution key, not the mathematical validity of the unqualified original prompt. The complete revealed source solution explicitly ends: Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\" The submitted I and II only was actually graded Incorrect on the already chosen W slot. II alone is source-supported; the ordinary improper tangent integral remains divergent, and the original solver confident=false is preserved. Study correction will explicitly qualify the principal-value convention." "bindings" {"problem" "49047404b809912edd9b4c04561e2bcd2276b36e555915b6e5e909abfb3780b0" "worked_solution" "869eb63171ef30eb97b